\section*{Polynomial state complementation of two-way NFAs}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 129; 2026-09-25), the asserted result is that some $n$-state two-way nondeterministic automata require exponentially many states for recognizing their complements, when the alphabet may grow.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 129; 2026-09-25), a small source automaton guesses a nonempty product of binary relations. Path diagrams of a hypothetical complement recognizer induce an order-reversing monoid image; corner reductions and a missing-set amplification force exponential rank loss and hence an exponential state bound.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The displayed question has a negative answer under the manuscript's stated construction. Its counterexample meets the conditions in this problem and defeats the universal assertion, so this is a full disproof claim. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 129; 2026-09-25).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 129; 2026-09-25), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: some $n$-state two-way nondeterministic automata require exponentially many states for recognizing their complements, when the alphabet may grow.

The selected statement includes the clause used for this problem.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{OneWayLiveness.lean}, \texttt{TwoWayComplementation.lean}, \texttt{TwoWayDeterminization.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.OneWayLiveness.main\_theorem}, \texttt{OAI.TwoWayComplementation.complementation\_lower\_bound\_finite}, \texttt{OAI.TwoWayComplementation.explicit\_family\_main}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

